 "name": "Abandoned Landscape Futures: Photo Ranking",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the photograph-ranking exercise in van der Zanden, Carvalho-Ribeiro & Verburg (2018), \"Abandonment landscapes: user attitudes, alternative futures and land management in Castro Laboreiro, Portugal\", Regional Environmental Change 18:1509–1520. Participants rank the landscapes that grow back after farming stops (oak forest, tall shrubland, low shrubland, Acacia encroachment and, in the valleys, plantations) from most to least attractive, once for the valleys (5 landscapes) and once for the plateau (4), and say why. Each picture pairs a close-up and a distant view, as in the paper. The photographs are the authors' field photographs from Online Resource 1 (Figs. 2 and 3; CC BY 4.0), used with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) only the photo-ranking section (section 3 of six) is included; the other sections' exact wording is not published, so user group, connection to agriculture, sex, age and place of residence are asked with short wording of our own; (2) the original was a 45-minute face-to-face questionnaire in Portuguese and English with 122 local inhabitants, visitors and experts; this is a short online version; (3) landscapes are shown under neutral letters in random order and ranked by tapping; (4) each close-up and distant photo pair is shown as one combined picture; the plateau photos are smaller in the source file.",
